# Tile-threading block-guard throughput: the #445 regression, and its fix

date: 2026-08-06
host: Apple M4 Pro (8 P-core + 4 E-core = 12 logical), macOS 26.5.2, aarch64
rustc: 1.97.1 (8bab26f4f 2026-07-14), stable
profile: release (lto = "fat", codegen-units = 1), default features
         (bitdepth_8 + bitdepth_16 — i.e. the CHECKED DisjointMut build).
         No `-C target-cpu=native`.

## nice level — read this before reproducing

NO `nice` WAS APPLIED TO ANY TIMED RUN. On Darwin a positive nice value maps the
process to background QoS (efficiency cores) and distorts wall clock by well
over an order of magnitude. `nice -n 19` was used for BUILDS only.

Arms are interleaved back-to-back per (vector, threads) cell, and their order is
ROTATED each round, so no arm systematically inherits the others' thermal wake.
Reported numbers are MEDIANS over every rep of every round (9 reps for the A/B
sweep, 6 for the three-arm sweeps), not single shots.

The harness records the 1-minute load average with every cell and refuses to
start while any process is above 25% CPU. Both guards exist because they were
needed: an orphaned background sweep and an editor's `cargo check` (844% CPU)
each silently invalidated an entire dataset during this session. Any cell whose
LOAD column is above ~3 should be treated as suspect.

## Commits compared

  before   0d350d1  (immediately before #445)
  after    a6a7e232 (#445 as merged on main)
  batched  the fix in this branch, on top of a6a7e232
  strided  a REJECTED intermediate attempt, kept only as evidence (see below)

All arms are builds of `examples/bench_ab_decode.rs`, which is committed so both
sides of a cross-commit comparison run identical harness code.

## Exact commands

  # vectors (from test-vectors/bench/photo_4k.avif, via avifdec + sips + avifenc)
  avifdec test-vectors/bench/photo_4k.avif src4k.png
  sips -Z 1024 src4k.png --out src1024.png ; sips -Z 256 src4k.png --out src256.png
  avifenc -q 70 -s 6 -y 444 [-d 10] --tilecolslog2 {0,2} --tilerowslog2 {0,1} <src>.png <out>.avif

  # build (nice is fine here — builds are not timed)
  CARGO_TARGET_DIR=<per-arm> RUSTC_WRAPPER=sccache \
    nice -n 19 cargo build --release --example bench_ab_decode -j 4

  # sweep
  caffeinate -i ./scripts/perf/ab_sweep.sh <vector_dir> <out.tsv> <rounds> \
    before=<bin> after=<bin> batched=<bin>
  python3 scripts/perf/ab_report.py <out.tsv>

## Data files

  tile_threading_guard_ab_2026-08-06.tsv.zst       before vs after, 3 rounds x 3 reps
  tile_threading_guard_strided_2026-08-06.tsv.zst  before vs after vs the REJECTED attempt
  tile_threading_guard_fix_2026-08-06.tsv.zst      before vs after vs the shipped fix

Columns: round, kind, arm, vector, threads, [rep, iters, ms_total, ms_per_frame].
`FAIL` rows record a cell whose decode failed rather than dropping it.

## Result 1 — the #445 regression, measured (after / before, median)

  vector           geom            t=1     t=2     t=4     t=8
  v256             256x144  8bpc   1.022   1.073   1.065   1.062
  v1024            1024x576 8bpc   1.007   1.076   1.082   1.078
  v4k_1tile        3840x2160 8bpc  1.010   1.043   1.045   1.048
  v1024_10b        1024x576 10bpc  1.001   1.008   1.002   1.008
  v4k_1tile_10b    3840x2160 10bpc 0.997   1.004   1.003   1.008
  v4k_8tile        3840x2160 8bpc  1.019   before PANICS at t>=2 (this is why #445 exists)
  v4k_8tile_10b    3840x2160 10bpc 0.994   1.001   0.977   0.970

* threads=1 is the control and is UNCHANGED (0.997-1.022). The compact path is
  gated on `tile_threading_active()`, which is false at n_tc == 1, so this is
  the expected result and it holds.
* 8-bit at threads>=2 costs +4.3% to +8.2%. That is the regression.
* 10-bit is unaffected (~1.00). #445 converted the aarch64 safe-SIMD sites whose
  hot paths are 8bpc; the 16bpc kernels do not reach them in this content.
* On genuinely multi-tile content the `before` arm cannot decode at all at
  threads>=2 — it panics with the overlapping-DisjointMut report #445 fixed. So
  for that column there is no baseline to regress against.

## Result 2 — where the cost is: guard bookkeeping, NOT the copy

A throwaway probe build kept both memcpys but took ONE contiguous guard instead
of `h` per-row guards (incorrect under tile threading — it reserves the gaps
again — but valid on the 1-tile vectors used to measure it):

  v1024,     threads=4:  before 50.2   after 54.2   probe 50.0
  v4k_1tile, threads=4:  before 562.7  after 586.0  probe 561.0

The probe is indistinguishable from baseline. The two memcpys are free; the
entire regression is the `2h` extra tracker lock round-trips per block.

`sample`-based profiles agree: `BorrowTracker::add_mut` is ~13% of decode wall
time in the baseline, and it is the second-hottest symbol in the binary after
`decode_coefs`.

## Result 3 — a REJECTED attempt, recorded so it is not retried

> **PARTIALLY SUPERSEDED 2026-08-13 (PR-triage round). It WAS retried, five days
> later, and it PAID — because the retry obeyed exactly the constraint this
> section ends on.** #505 built an exact strided-rectangle record and #513 made it
> the DEFAULT path on 2026-08-11 (`Cargo.toml` now carries `__lf_rect` and
> `__rows_rect` as declared no-ops saying so). The section below is still correct
> about ITS OWN two designs and about why they failed — do not put row geometry in
> `Bounds`, and do not add an indirection to the dense scan. What is now known and
> was not knowable here: a rectangle record whose scan cost is unchanged is worth
> **−2.0% to −2.5% wall at t=8** on the 1024-wide family from the CDEF seam alone,
> and −3.9% to −4.5% composed with `LfBlock::fill`'s (`docs/LAYOUT_CDEF.md` §4).
> So read "recorded so it is not retried" as "recorded so it is not retried THIS
> WAY". Two further corrections from later rounds bear on the numbers below:
> `main` now prices a count cut by **distinct shard lines visited**, not records
> filed (`docs/RECT_RECORDS.md`), and every t=1 figure within ~1.5% on a
> large-working-set cell sits inside a measured **code-placement lottery**
> (`docs/RECT_SHIP.md` §3, AGENT_BRIEF §6) — which the "+20% at threads=1" here is
> far outside, but the "1.19-1.27" tail is not.


Registering the block's 2-D shape as ONE strided tracker record (row_len +
row_stride, with exact strided-vs-dense overlap math) removes both the gap
reservation and the per-row lock traffic, and is the design the open #449 also
wants. It measured WORSE than the thing it replaced, twice:

  * v1: widening `Bounds` (16 -> 32 bytes) and the dense slot arrays with row
    geometry cost 1.6x on `add_mut` and ~20% on whole-frame decode, at
    threads=1, before the new code path was even reached.
  * v2: moving the geometry to a side table consulted only when a strided
    borrow is live still cost +19% (10-bit, threads>=2), because a strided
    borrow is live almost continuously under tile threading and every dense
    borrow then paid an out-of-line call. An inline containing-interval reject
    recovered the tiny vector but not the rest: 1.19-1.27 vs before.

The lesson is that the overlap scan is far too hot to carry 2-D math or an extra
indirection. Any future strided-record design has to leave the dense scan
byte-identical, which is the constraint the shipped fix satisfies by not
changing the scan at all.

## Result 4 — the shipped fix (batched / before, median)

Same per-row reservations as #445, registered ROW_BATCH (4) at a time under one
tracker lock instead of one lock round-trip per row. Every stored record is
still a plain interval, so the overlap scan is untouched.

  vector           geom            t=1     t=2     t=4     t=8
  v256             256x144  8bpc   1.003   1.046   1.040   1.039
  v1024            1024x576 8bpc   1.007   0.987   0.999   1.002
  v4k_1tile        3840x2160 8bpc  1.008   1.010   1.005   1.004
  v1024_10b        1024x576 10bpc  1.001   0.978   0.980   0.978
  v4k_1tile_10b    3840x2160 10bpc 0.995   0.988   0.986   0.982
  v4k_8tile_10b    3840x2160 10bpc 0.997   0.971   0.899   0.953

  v4k_8tile 8bpc (before cannot decode at t>=2; batched vs AFTER):
    t=2  471.0 vs 503.8  (0.94x)
    t=4  578.4 vs 676.2  (0.86x)
    t=8 1100.8 vs 1120.8 (0.98x)

The 8-bit regression is gone (1.076-1.082 -> 0.987-1.010 on v1024). Several
cells land BELOW the pre-#445 baseline, because the loopfilter and ipred compact
paths — which used per-row guards long before #445 — are batched too.

Residual: v256 at threads>=2 is still +4%. It is the smallest vector (1.2 ms per
frame), where per-frame fixed cost dominates and blocks are short enough that
batching four rows saves little. Not chased further.

## Result 5 — tile threading anti-scales, and that PREDATES #445

Independent of any of the above, and the more important number for anyone
choosing a thread count. Decode time vs threads on the shipped fix, 3840x2160:

  v4k_8tile   8bpc:  t=1 591  t=2 471 (1.25x)  t=4 578 (1.02x)  t=8 1101 (0.54x)
  v4k_8tile  10bpc:  t=1 665  t=2 529 (1.26x)  t=4 633 (1.05x)  t=8 1205 (0.55x)
  v4k_1tile   8bpc:  t=1 588  t=2 569        t=4 568          t=8 567

The `before` arm shows the same shape on 10-bit (t=1 667, t=2 544, t=4 705,
t=8 1265), so this is not caused by #445 or by the compact path.

* Peak is 2 threads, at ~1.25x, and only on multi-tile content.
* 4 threads is roughly a wash; 8 threads is nearly 2x SLOWER than 1 thread.
* Single-tile content gets nothing from any thread count, as expected — but it
  still pays the compact path, because `set_tile_threading` is a process-wide
  flag keyed on `n_tc > 1`, not on the stream's actual tile count.

The mechanism is the tracker's per-buffer spinlock: every borrow of a picture
plane takes it, so all tile workers serialise on one lock per plane and trade
its cache line. Fixing that means sharding or partitioning the tracker, which is
a larger change than this pass.

`managed::Settings::default()` already sets `threads: 1`, so the default
configuration is unaffected. The doc comment on that field currently says users
"can set threads=0 for better performance"; on this machine and this content
that is false for tile threading, and it should be re-worded when someone
touches it.

## Correctness

* Bit identity, full suite: `cargo test --release --no-fail-fast` on unmodified
  main and on this branch. 4945 `MISMATCH [tier]: <id> expected=<hex>
  actual=<hex>` triples extracted from each run; 4945 compared, 0 differed.
* Failing-test set: 34 on main, 34 on this branch, IDENTICAL by name (the
  pre-existing decode_cpu_levels / decode_md5_verify / decode_permutations /
  test_vq_suite_standalone failures). The set did not grow.
* `cargo test --release --test mt_stress` passes at threads 1/2/4/8/16, 5 trials
  each — the gate #445 exists for.
* Every sweep cell also carries a decoded-frame md5. Across all three arms, all
  7 vectors, all 4 thread counts: all agree, and no arm's output varies with
  thread count.
